cartridge "JAILBREAK/B.BLACKMER/1987"

' JAILBREAK
' A ColecoVision game based on "Jailbreak" by Bob Blackmer, Ahoy!
' February 1987 (Commodore 64 machine language at $C000, started by a
' two-line BASIC boot). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/jailbreak.dis), the game is built for the
' ColecoVision: a prison yard with dirt paths, nine brick cells with
' barred doors, a striped prisoner and a guard as smooth two-colour
' sprites, case files and a lawyer drawn in tiles, sound effects and a
' title tune.
'
' The game: you were framed. Nine cells stand in a 3 x 3 block, with paths
' between them. Seven cells hold a piece of evidence; your lawyer waits in
' the bottom right cell. Walk the paths, step into a cell through its door
' when the door is open, and pick up the evidence: each piece adds a word
' of YOU HAVE THE EVIDENCE TO CLEAR YOURSELF! at the top. With all seven,
' walk into the lawyer's cell and you are free. Doors open at random and
' now and then all slam shut at once. A guard walks the paths at random:
' if he catches you, you are back in the middle cell, every door slams
' shut and the guard walks faster (four speeds). The clock runs all the
' time: your score is your time, and the best one is kept.
'
' From the original (orig/): the 3 x 3 cells with doors on their left
' side, a 4 x 4 grid of path crossings; up and down only on a path column,
' left and right only on a path row, or into a cell from its door row when
' the door is open (and out again only through an open door); seven files
' in cells 1-4 and 6-8, the lawyer ('L' on the C64) in cell 9, the start
' in cell 5 with its door open and all the others shut; the guard picks a
' random direction at every crossing (any of the four, the way back too)
' and walks to the next one; caught: back to cell 5, guard speed +1 up to
' 4 (the player's own speed), all doors shut; random openings of one door
' at a time; a rare slam of every door (1 chance in 32 every 256 passes);
' the word list; the elapsed time (mm:ss, the C64's CIA clock); entering
' the lawyer's cell with fewer than seven files does nothing.
'
' Liberties: the player walks 2 pixels a frame and the guard 1/2, 1, 1 1/2
' and 2 (the C64's 4 pixels a loop against 1 to 4, run at about 50 loops a
' second, was too fast for the ColecoVision's smaller yard); doors open
' about twice as often (one door every two seconds or so, any of nine) so
' a player shut in a cell is not kept waiting for a minute; the walk into
' and out of a cell is animated (the C64 jumped); a short pause after a
' capture; corner help: holding a direction that is blocked slides you
' onto the nearest path within 10 pixels; diagonals work (the vertical
' part is tried first); a chime for each file, a click when a door opens,
' a fanfare when you are free; the best time is kept; the end shows your
' time, then the title (the C64 showed PRESS FIRE and started again).
' Hiding in a cell is safe, as on the C64, but the clock keeps running,
' so it never pays.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the stick walks. On the title, up and
' down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own
' xorshift random numbers.

